   <div class="cs-faq-list">

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>What is a bottom load centre spindle operator?</summary>
      <p>A bottom load centre spindle operator is a concealed automatic swing door operator installed within the door header. It drives the door through a vertical centre spindle that passes through the door panel.</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>What is the difference between a surface mount and a centre spindle operator?</summary>
      <p>A surface mount operator is installed visibly on the door or frame, while the bottom load centre spindle operator is concealed within the header and drives the door through a centre spindle. The appropriate system depends on the architectural design and installation requirements.</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>Which models are available in the Centre Spindle Series?</summary>
      <p>The Omega OA100-BL Centre Spindle Series includes CS-L, CS-R, CS-AA, CS-AM and CS-MA configurations for single and dual-door arrangements.</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>What is the difference between CS-L and CS-R?</summary>
      <p>CS-L is the left-hand centre spindle configuration, while CS-R is the right-hand configuration. Refer to the relevant technical drawing to confirm the correct handing for the door installation.</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>Are dual-door centre spindle configurations available?</summary>
      <p>Yes. The published dual configurations are CS-AA (Active/Active), CS-AM (Active/Monitor) and CS-MA (Monitor/Active).</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>How does the centre spindle drive work?</summary>
      <p>The centre spindle arrangement transfers the operator drive through a vertical spindle passing through the door panel. This is the defining drive arrangement of the Omega Centre Spindle Series.</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>Why choose a concealed centre spindle operator?</summary>
      <p>A concealed centre spindle operator can be considered where overhead clearance is restricted or where a clean, uninterrupted transom line is an architectural priority. It also avoids the appearance of a surface-mounted operator box.</p>
     </details>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-faq-item">
     <details>
      <summary>What is the maximum door weight capacity?</summary>
